			<div class="content" id="post-content-4833724">
									

<p>This&#8230;is a really strong newbie concept.</p>
<p>Okay. Warning. Rambley crit ahead. So please.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+Bear&nbsp;with&nbsp;me</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-Yeet</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I adore the emotional aspect and I'm a sucker for&#8230;how should I put it? 'Dead spirit super attached to living person' stories? Best way to put it?</p>
<p>I do have issues, though. First off&#8230;the topic of suicide. It's a difficult topic to handle, as it's <em>extremely</em> sensitive and one needs to pull it off well in order to make it work. I really like what you have here in concept, but I'm worried about the execution.</p>
<p>Also, go here<br />
<a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/task-forces#omega-0">http://www.scpwiki.com/task-forces#omega-0</a></p>
<p>And look at the Mobile Task Force called MTF Omega-0 (&quot;Ará Orún&quot;). In summary, it's a Mobile Task Force comprised of the spirits(?) of dead Foundation personnel.</p>
<p>Note: SCP Foundation storytelling is <em>all</em>headcanon, so you don't <em>have</em> to do anything with this MTF, but I figured someone would mention it later down the road, so I figured you'd need to know.</p>
<p>Also, I get it's just a hallucination and not actually a spirit, but still. I just wanted to share that MTF.</p>
<p>My last question is this: tell me why this needs to be an SCP and not a Tale.</p>
<p>A Tale, you have so much freedom to tell a story however you choose.</p>
<p>An SCP with its restrictive post-mortem emotionless scientific document format, the best way I can see this being told is a combination of interviews, handwritten notes, video footage of the MTFs behavior, perhaps some examples of on the field mission logs where the grieving MTF soldier starts losing his cool and goes nuts. That can all work perfectly, I admit but I'd want to see how you'd portray it.</p>
<p>In a Tale, you can accurately describe everything down to the nitty gritty of the MTF seeing his dead mate and such.</p>
<p>In the SCP format, all you'd be showing is someone going nuts from an outsider's perspective. The only anomalous bit would be the kicker in an addendum that someone else started hallucinating it.</p>
<p>Both have their strengths but I just want you to be aware of what storytelling tools you have.</p>
<p>Now, one final issue I have. This is a grave one. Extremely grave issue.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In benefit of maintaining resources, rather than promoting an adequate mental health, Foundation classifies it as an anomaly and moves on.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't see money as an issue to the Foundation. Also, this person's Mobile Task Force. I don't really buy them just shrugging him off like that.</p>
<p>The 'not an anomaly but emotions turn it into an anomaly' bit, I see working just fine. If anything, this can toy with the reader's expectations and make them wonder whether it really is a ghost or just a hallucination.</p>
<p>The careless attitude of the Foundation, I don't buy. Besides, I imagine they have on site mental health experts.</p>
<p>I don't know, maybe have him be checked out repeatedly by mental health experts and then have it fail and <em>then</em> have it be classified as an anomaly afterwards? After he commits suicide (which you'll still need to find a way to execute well.)</p>
<p>Simply saying 'screw it' and classifying an anomaly out of desire to save funds is so not Foundation.</p>
<p>Anywho, that ends my incredibly rambley crit. I apologise if some of this was a little unclear. If you need clarification, feel free to PM me.</p>
<p>I hope I helped and I bid you a lovely day.</p>
<p>👍</p>
</div>
</div>
