			<div class="content" id="post-content-4833854">
									

<p>Whoa, that was amazing. Thank you so much for such an extensive feedback. I will answer it without any order:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Some&nbsp;notes&nbsp;incoming</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Go</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<ul>
<li>I agree with the Foundation attitude to the problem not being 'saving money'. I think the obvious approach would instead be that it would be risky to keep the guy in the MTF squad, as it might keep endangering other squad mates due to his unpredictability. As the situation escalates and Samuel is emotionally stopped from performing any activity, that would be another reason to separate him from the group. (edited in the OP)</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>I did not know about MTF Omega-0. I have not read thoroughly about it yet, but my understanding is that it uploads 'memories' to the SCP intranet? I might believe that if Samuel is troubled in his mind, syncing his mind/soul/spirit to the Foundation intranet might actually threaten the stability of Omega-0. It could even act as a cybervirus for the performance of the Omega-0 squad.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>I understand that telling this story either as a Tale or SCP entails radically different structures. I can state two arguments why I think I would prefer to write this as an SCP file.
<ul>
<li>First, as a newbie writer I am not that confident yet on my strength in fully fleshing-out feelings of this kind in a Tale, without sounding exaggerated or hyperbolic. I am absolutely into subtlety, which ties with my second argument.</li>
<li>As you mentioned, one of the reasons why I did not write Adam's apparition to be real (aside from the mental health topic) is to play with the reader's expectations. Despite the clinical approach by the Foundation, the reader would nonetheless think that something is off. If we are telling the reader every time that Samuel is watching someone, she would be more prone to think that Samuel's right while the Foundation is missing something. As story progresses, amnestics result powerless and Samuel becomes physically constrained due to his mental strings, and so the reader would confirm her expectations.</li>
<li>One of the consequences of the SCP format is that the reader would actually never know if Adam's apparition was actually real or not. But I find that attractive, though. In this case, I think it would better work that the Foundation considers anomalous not 'Adam's apparition', but Samuel's mental problem, due to the resistance to amnestics.</li>
<li>Samuel's image becoming a memetic effect is something I am not quite sure, honestly. It may appear out of the blue, but it is another plot twist that I guess one does not see coming. If at all, one might expect Adam to appear to another MTF officer, not Samuel. But it might change the meaning of the whole writing by emphasizing the idea of Samuel's sorrow. Also, it rises the stakes for the Foundation, as it implies that what happened to Samuel might 'jump' to MTF officers and affect them, risking prospective MTF operations. The Foundation might establish as containment procedure that MTFs are to be monitored in case somebody shows any of the mentioned effects, isolate the guy to confirm it, and keep it alive for the time being.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>The idea I explained as 'Central Narrative' was intended to stand as background: it would be told as you mention via medical/psychological reports, video logs from the missions, etc. Specifically, I had in mind to include one file acting as a HRM initial report at the moment of entry of Samuel; and another file being an interview with Adam and Samuel's mentor in police academy detailing their closeness. This second one could be included into the HRM initial report as well.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>On Samuel's suicide, I understand it is a sensitive topic and difficult to pull off. The final twist requires him to die to make sense. A final addendum stating that Samuel appeared dead in his cell, then tying up with him appearing to another MTF officer seems too simple and anticlimactic. A suicide note seems also unconvincing and unoriginal, not to mention that he was reportedly unable to perform any action by that time.</li>
</ul>
<p>That was a long response, I think I did not miss anything. Again, thank you so much for your help!</p>
</div>
