																		Re: It Will Not Leave
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/zyn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1404533); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1404533&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607221448" alt="Zyn" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1404533)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/zyn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1404533); return false;" >Zyn</a></span> <span class="odate time_1607217379 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">06 Dec 2020 01:16</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4837437">
									

<p>Got a PM about this, stated I didn't like it and my review would be negative, was requested to look at this all the same. With that noted, here we go:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I'll be honest, I was rather immediately skeptical when I read that Adam and Samuel not only are childhood friends and joined police academy at the same time, but they were also recruited by the Foundation <em>and</em> placed on an MTF immediately. MTF agents are the Foundation's best of the best, and the teams are specialized for more than just generic combat&#8212;how on earth did both of these guys just immediately get put on an MTF team, let alone the same team?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The grief and sense of guilt pursuits Samuel to the point that starts to see Adam during his routine more often with time, to the point that jeopardizes other companions during some MTF operations. After a psychological test, he is given an amnestic to forget about his friend and stick to the MTF.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is where I as a reader start to lose interest in a full draft of this. It strikes me as odd that no one bothered addressing Samuel's trauma until <em>after</em> he started jeopardizing operations&#8212;surely the Foundation would have had him attend counseling as soon as he returned from the original mission? The &quot;psychological test&quot; involving the amnestic just seems incompetent and negligent; even if Samuel asked for his memory to be erased, he shouldn't have been allowed to continue with his MTF duties. It's also a huge hit to Task Force morale if the way the Foundation handles grief is to just force people to forget and chuck them back into the field.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>However, after some time without issues, during an intense MTF operation, Samuel provokes a containment breach as he claims that an unknown individual appeared to him.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>At this point the plotline just feels too contrived for me to take it seriously. Given the Foundation's judgment lapses thus far, as a reader I react with more &quot;well, what did they expect?&quot; and not &quot;oh no, that's terrible!&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Foundation demotes him and keeps him a study subject in order to prevent further failed operations, classifying Adam’s apparition as an SCP object.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I mean, I guess after two ill-advised doses of amnestics it's reasonable to guess that something anomalous is afoot, but I'm still wondering why Samuel didn't get any sort of therapeutic care during all of this.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Eventually, Samuel commits suicide.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I would downvote pretty immediately for this. It's overdramatic and entirely avoidable, and the usage of suicide as a plot point doesn't sit well with me. Granted, I myself headcanon the Foundation as extremely competent&#8230; if you really want to write a story where the Foundation thoroughly fails to help one of their personnel, I'm definitely not the right reviewer for such a piece.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>An MTF operative from Samuel’s former MTF squad is recorded in a tapped phone call claiming that he has spotted Samuel in his garden and is afraid of reporting this to the Foundation.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why would this MTF agent be scared of reporting that sighting? The Foundation can't do anything about what they don't know, and keeping knowledge of a potential anomalous effect secret would put everyone else the operative works with at risk.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Adam’s apparition to Samuel is not real. It is driven by strong feelings of guilt, grief and melancholy. [&#8230;] So to avoid jeopardizing future operations, Foundation classifies it as an anomaly. Samuel’s sorrow becomes a memetic effect.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I have a very difficult time taking this seriously. Emotions and mental symptoms associated with traumatic events are very real and I can't see the Foundation treating this case as an anomaly. The justification of &quot;they don't want to jeopardize future operations&quot; doesn't make sense (unless Samuel is just too irreplaceable) since Samuel wouldn't mess up operations if he was allowed to take time away to mourn in peace. The sorrow becoming a memetic effect also seems odd since there's no specific information being transferred here. Mind-affecting maybe, but I'm not seeing the memetics relation.</p>
<p>It also seems weird that the Foundation would decide to write up an uncontainable anomalous phenomenon that would cause more panic, rather than just getting the MTF staff some therapy.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>First, how even long-term friendship can deeply mark an individual;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This admittedly makes me curious as to why Adam and Samuel were placed on the same MTF team. They'd potential be emotional liabilities to each other. I can see why it has to be set up this way to commence the desired plot, but if the narrative focus on friendship is reliant on a contrived plot, the emotion might feel artificial and disingenuous.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>and second, the HRM policy of the Foundation regarding its MTF operatives, and the perils of mental health mismanagement.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Given that the MTF agents are the best of the best, I have trouble believing that the Foundation would mismanage Samuel's care to such a disastrous degree. Again, the incompetence of the Foundation feels more like authorial meddling than something that I personally would believe, and the core theme is difficult for me to take seriously as currently presented.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>As an aside, I admittedly have a lot of skepticism on this sort of story since I've volunteered at a psyc department, written a thesis on cancer patient trauma, and handled data for for a stress research lab. There's a wide body of research regarding grief and mourning (also one of my best friends lost a beloved dog recently. She says she still hears his barking sometimes. This isn't an abnormality) and the main roadblock I have is the Foundation being portrayed as, well&#8230; pretty clueless, it seems.</p>
<p>Again though, I tend to be more picky and demanding for scientific realism than most readers. There are plenty community members who enjoy stories where the Foundation messes up a lot; I just personally can't take those stories seriously enough to enjoy the read.</p>
<p>So far as suggestions for moving forward go, this is what I recommend:</p>
<ul>
<li>change the role assignment for the two. MTF raises more eyebrows than, say, security personnel (would make sense for someone with police work in their resume) or field agent.</li>
<li>have the Foundation provide therapy for Samuel after Adam dies.</li>
<li>make it clear that Samuel asks to be put on the amnestics. Have it just be the one dose.</li>
<li>don't have the Foundation write up the anomaly. Maybe have the document be something that Samuel was writing up himself, believing that he was affected by an anomaly because his grief broke through the amnestics.</li>
<li>don't end the piece with Samuel's suicide.</li>
<li>if you want to have the Foundation be really struggling to provide mental health care, have there be a clear and believable reason <em>why</em>. Remote site that is dangerous and inhospitable to therapists who are used to working in offices? Stubborn refusal of Samuel to take the counseling sessions seriously? Etc.</li>
</ul>
<p>I myself would have set this up as a tale. Do you really need to have this be an SCP article? You can consider writing a tale that has article inserts, like <a href="/revised-entry">Revised Entry</a> or <a href="/reality-check">Reality Check</a>.</p>

